# ---- the main-red burst back-walk and the suspected freeze (BH-1451, optional — ON is the default) ----
# 2026-09-22, 12:28-12:45Z. Main went red when #1394 merged. The hub's merge
# lanes then merged FIVE more PRs onto that red main while
# bin/main-red-detector.sh logged, every tick for 15+ minutes, "no completed run
# of a REQUIRED workflow on main — nothing decided". During a merge burst main
# advances every minute or two, so every run in the page is still in_progress:
# the streak arm needs two completed reds and the tip arm needs a completed red
# ON THE TIP, and a burst removes both kinds of evidence from the page. These
# four numbers are the two halves of the answer.
#
# The BURST BACK-WALK arm. When neither of the older arms can decide and the
# head still has required runs in flight, the detector walks BACK through the
# main shas the runs page holds and decides from the newest COMPLETED required
# run it finds. A red there writes flags/main-red-<owner>-<repo> with the sha it
# JUDGED, the head it is behind and how far behind, and pages once.
# 0 = only the streak and tip arms fire, which is byte-for-byte the pre-BH-1451
# decision — including the deeper re-read below. A rollback switch, not a tuning
# knob; only the literal 0 disarms it.
#MAIN_RED_BURST_BACKWALK=0
# How many DISTINCT main shas back the walk may reach. The ticket's floor is 5:
# the 2026-09-22 burst put three merges onto main inside four minutes, so a
# depth of 1 or 2 would have been blind to exactly the run that could decide.
# Depth 0 is the tip arm's business and this arm never claims it. Raise it on a
# repo that merges faster than the hub does; a walk that reaches past the last
# few commits is deciding from history nobody is pushing on any more.
#MAIN_RED_BURST_DEPTH=5
# Runs fetched on the ONE extra read a tick makes when its first page
# (MAIN_RED_PER_PAGE, 20) holds no completed required run at all and something
# required is still in flight. That is the incident's own shape: twenty runs on
# main, every one of them started in the last few minutes. The read happens only
# on a tick that would otherwise decide nothing, and never on a page with
# nothing pending, so a repo with a quiet main pays for it exactly never.
#MAIN_RED_BURST_PER_PAGE=100
# The SUSPECTED FREEZE in bin/main-red-pr-pair.sh. That lane sees a red main
# through the PRs — two open PRs failing the same job with the same first error
# — hours before main's own history says so, and until BH-1451 it wrote no flag,
# so on 2026-09-22 it alerted at 12:40Z and could not stop a merge. Now, when a
# pair fires AND the detector's own state/main-red/<slug>.verdict says
# decision=none, it raises the same flags/main-red-<slug> every merge lane
# already reads (reason=pr-pair-suspected, owner=main-red-pr-pair) and clears it
# on the first complete look that finds no pair. It never overrules a detector
# that HAS decided (red or green), and an ABSENT verdict file freezes nothing.
# 0 = the lane alerts exactly as it did before BH-1451 and writes no flag. A
# freeze it already raised is still cleared at 0, so the switch cannot strand
# one. Only the literal 0 disarms it.
#MAIN_RED_PAIR_FREEZE=0
